WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:08.700
You would not be watching me, here, on YouTube,
if it wasn’t for the zombie.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.330
I first saw Night of the Living Dead and Scooby-Doo
on Zombie Island around when I was 8

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:18.340
(which was either a questionable parenting
choice or the best one ever) and since then,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.850
the idea of the reanimated corpse fascinates
me—and yes, I recognize the potential red

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:24.850
flags here.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.770
I pursued my degree so that I could study
the undead and wrote my dissertation about

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:30.770
them.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:35.649
And now, I’m going to take what I’ve learned
and dig even deeper into the long and complex

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:37.769
history of the zombie.

00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:42.489
Zombies can be broken down into three significant
types, and this October, I’m dedicating

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:45.670
an entire episode for each one of our undead
friends.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.610
We’ll talk about the origins of each of
these zombie types throughout history and

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:56.629
uncover why it continues to hold such a special
place in our hearts—or should I say brains.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:02.059
And along the way, I’ll be talking to some
experts to get some special insight.

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:07.570
This three part YouTube series will culminate in a one hour documentary airing on PBS.  

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:12.259
So let’s kick it off with the origins of
the entire zombie lore, going all the way

00:01:12.259 --> 00:01:17.520
back to early West African spiritual practices.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.009
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:26.310
You can’t talk about the origins of the
zombie without talking about the spiritual

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.810
practice of Vodou.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.679
Vodou is practiced throughout the world, and
the concept of the zombi was born out of this

00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:33.679
religion.

00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:38.409
Voodoo is different from Vodou although even
now, the lines between them are consistently

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:40.680
blurred in popular culture.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.690
While one could argue that Vodou is the precursor
of Voodoo, it’s important to understand

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.720
that they are distinct and separate religions.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.070
But the original Vodou zombi is a zombie you
might not recognize.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:58.549
We need to look back more than four centuries
— to early African spiritual practices.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:04.009
Those legends originated in part from people
of West Africa, when men, women, and children

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:09.000
were forcibly taken from their homes, enslaved,
and shipped around the world.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.910
The Spanish shipped enslaved people to the
island of Hispaniola as early as 1502.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:20.300
In the 17th century, the French began establishing
colonies on the island as well, and by 1697,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:25.610
they had obtained control over the western
third —what is now Haiti —and named it

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:26.610
Saint-Domingue.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:31.160
Over the course of the 18th century, France
established the trade settlements of Les Cayes

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:32.170
and Port-au-Prince.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:37.600
With the demand for sugar, tobacco, spice,
and coffee in Europe rising, many enslaved

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.230
Africans ended up on plantations in Haiti
where the crops could grow in abundance—bolstering

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.740
France’s economy and becoming crucial to
its success as a world power.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:52.400
By the late 1780s, Saint-Domingue boasted
one third of the total people of the Atlantic

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.480
slave trade; and more than 90% of the island’s
population was enslaved.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.930
The African people on Saint-Domingue came
from vastly different geographic, cultural,

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.010
and spiritual backgrounds.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.850
Forced together in Haiti under the horrors
of slavery, they developed a new religion

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:12.850
to unite their often disparate views, beliefs,
and experiences.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:17.480
Integral to the Vodou religion is the belief
that the human body is merely flesh manipulated

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.040
by the two spiritual elements that make up
the soul—the ti-bon-anj and the gros bon

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.040
ange.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.430
The ti-bon-anj, also called the petit bon
ange, is the part of the individual able to

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:33.120
contact spirits called loas, as well as memory,
authority, and consciousness.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.460
Essentially, it is their personality, their
uniqueness.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:42.690
The gros bon ange controls the motor functions
of the body, allowing it to move and breathe.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.870
Each of these elements can be stolen.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.830
For practitioners of Vodou, death is a process
that takes time.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:54.280
The soul takes days to move on after death,
and until the process is complete, both parts

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.010
of the soul are at risk of wandering, or even
capture.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.370
This is where the zombi comes in.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:05.220
While the majority of Vodou priests and priestesses
use their ability to commune with and influence

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.050
the spirits in a positive way, a spiritual
leader who uses their talents for malicious

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.800
intent is often called a bokor.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.440
However, it’s important to note that others,
like Voodoo Priest Divine Prince Ty Emmecca,

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.260
argue that bokor is a derogatory term rooted
in prejudice.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.290
It's assumed when you say all, I've only known
it as negative.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:33.930
We're looking at Haiti and they use it to
Bokor and Haiti, which can be negative.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.580
They promote themselves in a very negative
way,&nbsp;

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:42.350
I want it clear that I'm not trying to represent
Haitian Vodou, that I'm indeed Louisiana,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.460
Mississippi Delta, West African voodoo.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.790
So that's another reason why I adopt an earned
the right to use Bokor

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:55.710
And so many words, including voodoo were bastardized.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:01.190
These names, these titles, these lineages,
and we still fight over them today, particularly

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:02.960
in social media.&nbsp;

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.660
Regardless of what the malicious Vodou practitioners
are called, they are identified by their willingness

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:12.530
to trap that part of the soul that houses
free will, the ti-bon-anj, in a bottle.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:18.310
It was believed that whoever possesses the
bottle controls the body of the deceased person.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:23.960
Many Haitians and Vodou practitioners believe
to this day that zombis are real, albeit rare.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:29.550
It’s important to note, however, that Haitian
zombis look nothing like those seen in today’s

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.150
popular culture.&nbsp;

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:37.730
Vodou zombis are simply shells: mindless workers
with no emotions, no ability to feel pain,

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.920
completely subservient to the whims of their
master.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.250
They may be brought to this form by administering
a poison that causes them to enter a coma-like

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:49.310
state in which they are still alive but appear
dead.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:55.550
The victim is passed off as a corpse, buried,
and then exhumed from the grave as a zombi.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:06:00.310
Zombification took root as an allegory for
colonialism, imperialism, and oppression.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:05.880
After all, is it not a threat worse than death,
the idea that one’s soul could also be enslaved,

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.389
and neither it nor the body could be free?&nbsp;

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:22.440
There's a notion that, well, in the slave
system, you have no power, right?

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:28.110
Your property, your own, your master has complete
control of your destiny, but voodoo gives

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:34.930
you the chance to exert a power over your enslaver that there's

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:38.730
no social parallel for.&nbsp;

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:43.960
The Haitian practitioners of Vodou did not
fear the zombi itself, as modern audiences

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:49.830
fear the flesh-eating zombies who pursue them
across the silver screen and in their nightmares.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:53.480
The Haitian slaves were terrified of becoming
a zombi.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.750
Voodoo is it's really a creation of, out of

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:03.570
slavery and enslavement, where they tried
to strip away ethnic groups.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:04.570
What makes you unique?

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:08.000
What makes you individual, you, 
you all are just product.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:09.889
And so that's where it became important.&nbsp;

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:16.460
From 1791 to 1804, enslaved Africans revolted
against their French oppressors.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:21.990
The violent uprisings eventually led to the
declaration of Haitian independence in 1804,

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.520
and slavery was officially abolished there
in 1805.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.010
Vodou further evolved in the United States
when the practices of the enslaved Haitian

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:34.930
people—brought to the States by French slave
owners during the Haitian revolution—were

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:36.240
subjected to new influences,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.750
So the Voodoo came with them.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:44.760
The gumbo mix of West African tradition with
indigenous tradition, and of course those

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:52.490
Euro centric tradition that we, in some cases
were forced to take on, but eventually adapted

00:07:52.490 --> 00:08:00.460
as sort of a cover, and a symbolic representation of something unique to us.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.840
With Voodoo, people want to put it all into
one box.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:07.370
It has to be one thing, either this is authentic
voodoo, or it's not authentic voodoo, if

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:08.680
there are black people, then this is voodoo.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:12.500
If they're white people that it's not, if
they're rich people that it doesn't count.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.979
Vodou was only practiced by poor people in
Haiti.

00:08:14.979 --> 00:08:20.889
I wish people understood that Voodoo was just
as complicated as every other American religion

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:22.940
and treated it that way.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:27.260
At the start of the 20th -century the zombie
was still largely unknown to the American

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:33.820
public—but that all changed when in 1915
the United States military occupied Haiti.&nbsp;

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:38.099
As American journalists and Marines returned
from Haiti, they brought stories of the island

00:08:38.099 --> 00:08:43.409
with them, including stories of the living
dead —their exaggerated interpretations

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:45.449
of Haitian zombis.

00:08:45.449 --> 00:08:51.730
One infamous example is William Seabrook’s
sensational 1929 travelogue, The Magic Island.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:56.740
He described these “dead men working”
as expressionless, with blank eyes like that

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:02.180
of a corpse.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:11.790
Seabrook is the one who really, I think brings
this notion of Voodoo, the vulgar Voodoo v-o-o-d-o-o

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:13.589
back to the States.

00:09:13.589 --> 00:09:24.629
He claims that he has gone to Haiti and he
has watched this sort of primal, wicked, lustful,

00:09:24.629 --> 00:09:30.199
um, movement dance, and it's sensuous and
it's uninhibited.

00:09:30.199 --> 00:09:33.440
And this is saying a little bit about Seabrook,
too.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.240
And so this wasn't entirely out of the scope
of what we already had a taste for.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.430
So he's just expanding that.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.510
And we clearly had an appetite for these kinds
of stories.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:47.209
Haiti came to be seen as a place of black
magic.

00:09:47.209 --> 00:09:52.629
The books and newspaper reports were bad enough,
but America’s perception of voodoo and Vodou

00:09:52.629 --> 00:09:58.170
got worse in 1932 with the release of the
first feature length zombie movie—White

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:03.879
Zombie, in which a love-sick man obtains the
object of his affections by poisoning her

00:10:03.879 --> 00:10:07.990
and bringing her back to life as a mindless
zombie.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:11.279
All of these early films are about, it's really
about lust.

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:12.310
It's about love.

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:18.490
It's about, um, someone who is as yet untouchable
and how can I get this person?

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:24.259
Well, what does this mean in the 19 teens,
the twenties or thirties, the forties, when

00:10:24.259 --> 00:10:34.000
the thing that is moving about, um, in some
ways, um, uninhibited is black.&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:41.059
It is important to note that the first zombie
ever to grace the silver screen was not Black.

00:10:41.059 --> 00:10:42.949
Neither was the bokor.

00:10:42.949 --> 00:10:48.699
White Zombie marks the first instance of white
bodies being subjected to zombification.&nbsp;

00:10:48.699 --> 00:10:50.119
That's the heightened threat.

00:10:50.119 --> 00:10:52.350
So white zombie is a great example of this.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:58.689
Although there isn't as much of an overt representation
of blacks as zombies, right?

00:10:58.689 --> 00:11:03.679
But there's still this permeation that this
evil, the wickedness of Haiti comes out of

00:11:03.679 --> 00:11:07.680
voodoo and has infected in even white people,
right?

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.939
Who are, are now zombies walking around.&nbsp;

00:11:10.939 --> 00:11:16.309
White Zombie’s release occurred the same
year that American pulp magazine Strange Tales

00:11:16.309 --> 00:11:20.860
published the first zombie narrative set in
the United States, in where else?

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:23.259
A plantation just outside of New Orleans.

00:11:23.259 --> 00:11:28.921
The 1932 story, “The House in the Magnolias,”
by August Derleth, &nbsp;not only demonizes people

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:32.250
from Haiti—it shows lingering fears in America&nbsp;

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:37.970
by further emphasizing the horrors of slavery
as well as equating Black bodies with monstrosity.

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:43.449
A painter from Chicago visiting New Orleans
discovers a beautiful old house of white wood

00:11:43.449 --> 00:11:46.779
“buried in magnolias.”&nbsp;

00:11:46.779 --> 00:11:51.170
Upon knocking on the door, he is confronted
with a Black woman with a gray cast to her

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:57.390
skin, and eyes “like deep black pools, bottomless,
inscrutable, and yet at the same time oddly

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.110
dull.”

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:04.149
She remains mute despite his questions, staring
at him unmoving.

00:12:04.149 --> 00:12:09.889
A beautiful Creole woman interrupts, and states
that she is the niece of the plantation owner,

00:12:09.889 --> 00:12:15.630
Aunt Abby, whom she describes as a “Haitian”
who left the island some years ago.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.779
She grants him permission to paint the house,
but on the conditions that he never brings

00:12:19.779 --> 00:12:24.939
anyone else to the plantation, never tells
anyone he’s there, and never gives Aunt

00:12:24.939 --> 00:12:27.850
Abby any indication he’s on the property.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:33.910
He is forbidden from going anywhere inside
the house besides the ground floor.&nbsp;

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:38.660
But his curiosity gets the best of him and,
under the cover of darkness, he explores the

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:44.889
house and its surrounding land, where he finds
Black people working the fields in utter silence.

00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:50.010
After pressuring Aunt Abby’s niece, he learns
from her that in Haiti “there are many strange

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:54.519
beliefs” like the “zombi”—"a dead
man who has been brought out of his grave

00:12:54.519 --> 00:12:56.399
and made to live again and to work.”

00:12:56.399 --> 00:13:00.879
Aunt Abby knows how to create these zombis
and keeps the slaves, all Black people, in

00:13:00.879 --> 00:13:03.230
the cellars during the day.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:07.351
The niece says that if they are given salt
“they will know they are dead and will find

00:13:07.351 --> 00:13:09.740
their way back to their graves.”

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:13.050
The painter and the niece resolve to free
these people.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:18.040
They feed the zombis candy with salt, and
one by one, they “wail terribly” and run

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:19.470
off into the night.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:23.899
The next day, the house is discovered burned
to the ground with Aunt Abby inside.

00:13:23.899 --> 00:13:29.379
The newspapers report the discovery of putrefied
remains in the half-opened graves of slaves

00:13:29.379 --> 00:13:32.580
“whose bodies had been stolen long years
ago—

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:37.191
and the curious detail that the graves had
been half dug by bare fingers, as if dead

00:13:37.191 --> 00:13:40.100
hands seek the empty coffins below!”

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:45.259
The blank stares of the first zombies in literature
and film were like blank slates upon which

00:13:45.259 --> 00:13:48.670
White Americans could write their own anxieties
and fears.

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:55.739
In the 1930s, you're justifying Jim Crow and
segregation so much like you're using race

00:13:55.739 --> 00:13:58.889
and religion to justify occupation and Haiti.

00:13:58.889 --> 00:14:02.649
You're using something similar to justify
segregation in New Orleans.

00:14:02.649 --> 00:14:10.559
I would argue that the zombie mythology and
that notion of black religion producing these

00:14:10.559 --> 00:14:17.589
moral, uh, evils is tied to that notion of
race and backwardness and savagery that is

00:14:17.589 --> 00:14:22.149
used to justify keeping black people in the
position you want them to be in, whether that

00:14:22.149 --> 00:14:27.470
be a kind of neocolonialism in Haiti or segregation
in new Orleans.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:32.649
The Haitian zombi ceded relevance to a more
violent version of the undead during the mid-20th

00:14:32.649 --> 00:14:33.649
century.

00:14:33.649 --> 00:14:38.769
But popular culture turned its attention back
to the original Vodou zombi in the 1980s

00:14:38.769 --> 00:14:40.860
&nbsp;
In The Serpent and the Rainbow, Wade Davis

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:45.290
wrote about his travels to Haiti to study
Vodou and the claims that its practitioners

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:50.850
were capable of creating real zombis through
the use of locally developed poisons.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:54.439
Davis claimed to have discovered two “zombie
powders,”

00:14:54.439 --> 00:14:58.009
one for causing the appearance of death and
the other for reviving the victim.

00:14:58.009 --> 00:15:04.069
He was able to obtain 8 samples of “before”
powders, all of which he claimed included

00:15:04.069 --> 00:15:09.470
tetrodotoxin (TTX), a psychoactive substance
taken from a unique Haitian pufferfish.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:15.420
However, later scientific studies of these
samples showed little to no tetrodotoxin present.

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.216
So the jury’s still out on that one

00:15:17.216 --> 00:15:24.242
I don't discuss it because, one, it is taboo. It's dangerous. 

00:15:24.242 --> 00:15:27.650
That information is guarded, is protected. 

00:15:27.650 --> 00:15:32.802
It's only shared with those qualified to handle that information.

00:15:32.802 --> 00:15:38.839
So when you start talking about zombies and the making of zombies, that's a tricky conversation. 

00:15:38.839 --> 00:15:42.689
Davis’s book sparked a renewed interest
in Vodou and the original zombi.

00:15:42.689 --> 00:15:48.420
It had enough of a public impact that Wes
Craven directed a film adaptation - the movie

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:52.980
aimed to prove that Vodou was a real religion—or
so Craven claimed.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:56.170
In reality Vodou was misrepresented as the
stuff of horror again.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:16:04.899
Again, it makes it very difficult to operate
a new traditions and feel your humanity is

00:16:04.899 --> 00:16:07.060
being acknowledged.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:10.660
People will acknowledge the voodoo before
they all acknowledged my humanity.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:15.509
People acknowledge whether it be as a fear
of the Voodoo or their need of something that

00:16:15.509 --> 00:16:23.269
they think the Voodoo can provide, but they're
still not humanizing me or the tradition in

00:16:23.269 --> 00:16:26.720
a way that we see everyday.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:32.019
Zombies may have their nascency in the spiritual
practices of the African diaspora, but popular

00:16:32.019 --> 00:16:36.929
culture’s very liberal interpretations of
the original idea mutated the creature into

00:16:36.929 --> 00:16:39.019
something else entirely.&nbsp;

00:16:39.019 --> 00:16:40.720
&nbsp;
In the next episode, we’ll look at how the

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:45.529
enslaved body, both in mind, spirit, and soul,
became the flesh-eating undead.

00:16:45.529 --> 00:16:48.939
I’ll give you a hint—"they’re coming
for you Barbara.”

